.TH AUTO-TLP 1 "2026" "auto-tlp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
auto-tlp \- generate a hardware-specific TLP configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auto-tlp
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B auto-tlp
scans the machine it runs on (CPU and its frequency scaling driver, batteries,
NVMe/SATA/HDD storage, GPUs, PCIe, Wi-Fi/Ethernet/Bluetooth, USB, audio and
more) by reading
.IR /sys ,
.I /proc
and DMI data, and prints a TLP configuration that contains only the settings
the detected hardware actually supports.
.PP
Scanning is strictly read-only. Nothing is written unless
.B \-\-output
or
.B \-\-install
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " " \fINAME\fR
Tuning profile: \fBbalanced\fR (default), \fBperformance\fR or \fBbattery\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-start\-thresh " N"
Battery start-charge threshold, 0-99. Overrides the profile default.
.TP
.BI \-\-stop\-thresh " N"
Battery stop-charge threshold, 1-100. Overrides the profile default.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the configuration to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output (\fB\-\fR means standard output).
.TP
.B \-\-install
Write the configuration to \fI/etc/tlp.d/99-auto-tlp.conf\fR (requires root).
An existing file is backed up to \fI99-auto-tlp.conf.bak\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Remove \fI/etc/tlp.d/99-auto-tlp.conf\fR (requires root).
.TP
.B \-\-apply
With \fB\-\-install\fR or \fB\-\-uninstall\fR: run \fBtlp start\fR afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Compare the generated configuration with the installed one and print the differences:
\fB+\fR new setting, \fB\-\fR setting that would disappear, \fB~\fR changed value.
Exit status is 1 if there are differences, 0 if there are none.
.TP
.BI \-\-against " FILE"
With \fB\-\-diff\fR: compare with \fIFILE\fR instead of the installed configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " LIST"
Generate only these comma-separated sections.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " LIST"
Leave out these comma-separated sections.
Sections: \fBgeneral\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBplatform\fR, \fBgraphics\fR, \fBstorage\fR, \fBpcie\fR,
\fBradios\fR, \fBusb\fR, \fBaudio\fR, \fBkernel\fR, \fBbattery\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FMT"
Output format: \fBconf\fR (default, native TLP config), \fBnix\fR (a NixOS \fBservices.tlp.settings\fR snippet)
or \fBjson\fR (flat object of the active settings). \fB\-\-install\fR, \fB\-\-uninstall\fR and \fB\-\-diff\fR need \fBconf\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-sysroot " DIR"
Scan the directory tree \fIDIR\fR instead of the live system. No host commands are run and
\fB\-\-install\fR/\fB\-\-uninstall\fR are refused. Used by the test-suite; the \fBAUTO_TLP_SYSROOT\fR
environment variable does the same.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-report
Print the detected hardware report instead of a configuration.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
With \fB\-\-report\fR, also list every PCI and USB device, sensors and the kernel command line.
.TP
.B \-\-terse
Omit explanatory comments from the generated configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-tlp\-version " X.Y"
Generate for this TLP version instead of the detected one. TLP 1.11 and newer use the profile-style key
names (\fB_ON_PRF\fR, \fB_ON_BAL\fR, \fB_ON_SAV\fR); older versions use \fB_ON_AC\fR and \fB_ON_BAT\fR.
When the version cannot be determined the classic names are used, because every TLP version accepts them.
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Use a built-in sample laptop (hybrid-graphics ThinkPad with NVMe, SATA SSD and HDD) instead of scanning this
machine, to preview the full range of output. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-install\fR or \fB\-\-uninstall\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH PROFILES
.TP
.B balanced
TLP-like defaults: turbo off on battery, 75-80% charge limit.
.TP
.B performance
Turbo stays on in battery mode, performance-leaning EPP, no charge limit.
.TP
.B battery
Maximum runtime and longevity: lower CPU cap on battery, 40-80% charge limit,
aggressive PCIe power saving.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tlp.d/99-auto-tlp.conf
Drop-in written by \fB\-\-install\fR. Settings that are active in
\fI/etc/tlp.conf\fR take precedence over drop-ins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success (with \fB\-\-diff\fR: no differences),
.B 1
with \fB\-\-diff\fR when there are differences,
.B 2
on invalid usage or when a file cannot be written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tlp (8),
.BR tlp\-stat (8),
.BR tlp.conf (5)
